meta {
  name: 13 - Issues Pagination Walk Page 2
  type: graphql
  seq: 13
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}
  body: graphql
  auth: inherit
}

body:graphql {
  query IssuesPaginationWalkPage2 {
    issues(first: 2, includeArchived: true, orderBy: updatedAt, after: "{{issues_page1_cursor}}") {
      nodes { id updatedAt }
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
    }
  }
}

script:pre-request {
  // Conditional fallback for out-of-order runs. NOT a `vars:pre-request` default, which
  // would be reassigned on every run and clobber the cursor request 12 captured.
  if (!bru.getVar('issues_page1_cursor')) {
    bru.setVar('issues_page1_cursor', '86eeb50c-332c-46a7-bebf-a0b67dc346bf');
  }
}

docs {
  Reference: https://linear.app/developers/pagination

  Page 2 of the walk started in `12 - Issues Pagination Walk`. Run that request first in a
  fresh session so `issues_page1_cursor` is populated; otherwise the pre-request script
  falls back to the cursor observed on 2026-07-30.

  **Limitation / observed finding** (confirmed 2026-07-30): completes the walk cleanly.

  - page 1 (`first: 2`)            -> `adbc7deb…`, `86eeb50c…`  `hasNextPage: true`
  - page 2 (`after: 86eeb50c…`)    -> `4937bc7e…`, `431c9f95…`  `hasNextPage: false`
  - page 3 (`after: 431c9f95…`)    -> `[]`, `hasNextPage: false`, **`endCursor: null`**

  Concatenating pages 1 and 2 reproduces the unpaged 4-id result in the same order, with no
  gap, duplicate, or reorder. The pagination machinery is contiguous and stable below the
  cap.

  **Completion signal for the connector: `pageInfo.hasNextPage == false`.** Stop there —
  do NOT loop until an empty page. The final page arrives WITH data and
  `hasNextPage: false` in the same response, so an empty-page-terminated loop would spend
  one wasted request per sweep per stream. `endCursor` going null on the exhausted page is
  a secondary signal and must not be relied on alone: it is null on a legitimately empty
  result set too (which is the normal steady state for an incremental sweep that finds no
  changes).

  **Not verified — cap-tail truncation.** Whether a >250-row result set capped at 250
  truncates the SORT TAIL rather than an arbitrary middle row cannot be observed here: it
  needs more than 250 rows in one collection, i.e. bulk seeding, which is out of scope
  (the skill's ~10-request seeding ceiling). We rely on Linear's documented keyset
  pagination contract for that boundary. The risk is contained regardless: with
  `sort: updatedAt Ascending` + `after`, the connector walks pages rather than jumping
  offsets, so it never skips past unread rows even if a page is short.
}

example {
  name: 200 — page 2, hasNextPage=false, walk reassembles

  request: {
    url: https://api.linear.app/graphql
    method: POST
    mode: graphql

    body:graphql: {
      query IssuesPaginationWalkPage2 {
        issues(first: 2, includeArchived: true, orderBy: updatedAt, after: "{{issues_page1_cursor}}") {
          nodes { id updatedAt }
          pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  response: {
    headers: {
      x-complexity: 4
      x-ratelimit-requests-limit: 2500
      x-ratelimit-requests-remaining: 2488
      x-ratelimit-complexity-limit: 3000000
      x-ratelimit-complexity-remaining: 2999996
    }

    status: {
      code: 200
      text: OK
    }

    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "data": {
            "issues": {
              "nodes": [
                {
                  "id": "86eeb50c-332c-46a7-bebf-a0b67dc346bf",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z"
                },
                {
                  "id": "431c9f95-fe23-44cb-9391-f4880c51bede",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z"
                }
              ],
              "pageInfo": {
                "hasNextPage": false,
                "endCursor": "431c9f95-fe23-44cb-9391-f4880c51bede"
              }
            }
          }
        }
      '''
    }
  }
}
